# MANET Project Context

## What This Is

A complete software suite that turns ARM64 SBCs (Rock 3A, Raspberry Pi 5, CM4) into self-forming mesh network nodes using **batman-adv** (Layer 2) over **802.11ax/s/ah** radios. Nodes coordinate via **Alfred** gossip to handle IP allocation, channel selection, service elections, and partition healing — all without central infrastructure.

## Hardware & Radio Architecture

Nodes can have any combination of these radios:

| Radio Type | Driver | Role | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| External AX card (mt7915e) | `mt7915e` | Mesh backhaul | Dual-band: one 2.4 GHz + one 5 GHz radio. Primary mesh transport. |
| Onboard WiFi (Broadcom) | `brcmfmac` | EUD Access Point | Cannot do mesh. Present on RPi 5 / some boards. Always used as AP when available. |
| HaLow (Morse Micro) | `morse` | Mesh backhaul | Sub-GHz 802.11ah. Long range, low throughput. Uses `wpa_supplicant_s1g`. |

### AP Selection Priority

1. **Non-mesh onboard radio** (brcmfmac) — first choice, frees both AX radios for mesh
2. **5 GHz AX radio** — last resort when no onboard WiFi exists; sacrificed from mesh duty
3. **Never**: HaLow or 2.4 GHz AX

### Interface Naming Convention

Interfaces are pinned via systemd `.link` files (by MAC) in `/etc/systemd/network/10-wlanX.link`. Contiguous naming: mesh radios first (wlan0=2.4 GHz, wlan1=5 GHz), then HaLow, then non-mesh. Driver load order varies between boards, so every radio must have a `.link` file to prevent naming races.

## Key Network Stack

```text
EUD clients ──► wlan2 (hostapd AP) ──► br0 (bridge) ◄── bat0 (batman-adv)
                                                              │
                                            ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
                                          wlan0             wlan1            wlan_halow
                                       (2.4 GHz mesh)    (5 GHz mesh)     (sub-GHz mesh)
                                      wpa_supplicant     wpa_supplicant   wpa_supplicant_s1g
```

- **bat0**: batman-adv virtual interface (BATMAN_V algorithm)
- **br0**: bridge containing bat0 + AP interface; carries the mesh IPv4/IPv6
- **end0**: Ethernet (renamed from eth0); DHCP client or gateway depending on uplink

## Gossip & Coordination

All mesh coordination flows through Alfred (Layer 2 multicast):

`encoder.py` (protobuf → base64) → `alfred -s 65` → mesh → `alfred -r 65` → `decoder.py` → `/var/run/mesh_node_registry`

The registry drives IP allocation, gateway selection, service elections, channel elections, and partition healing. Scripts never talk to each other directly — they read/write the shared registry.

## Node Manager Modes

Controlled by `acs=` in `/etc/mesh.conf`:

- **Static** (`node-manager-static.sh`): Simple loop — publish status, build registry, run IP manager and service elections. No RF scanning or channel changes.
- **ACS** (`node-manager-acs.sh`): Full orchestrator — adds RF scanning, channel elections, tourguide partition healing, limp mode (jamming response), and quorum checking.

## Web Interfaces on Nodes

- **Port 80** (`mesh-status.py`): Topology visualization, JSON API at `/api/data` (full mesh) and `/api/local` (this node only). Mobile-friendly.
- **Port 8081** (`perf-dashboard.py`): Performance testing UI — iperf, ping, WiFi/HaLow controls, data export.

## Key Paths on Nodes

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `/etc/mesh.conf` | Node configuration (SSID, key, network CIDR, EUD mode, services) |
| `/var/lib/mesh_if` | List of mesh-role interfaces (one per line) |
| `/var/lib/halow_if` | HaLow interfaces |
| `/var/lib/no_mesh_if` | Non-mesh interfaces (AP candidates) |
| `/var/lib/ap_interface` | Selected AP interface name |
| `/var/lib/iface_map` | Runtime name mapping |
| `/var/run/mesh_node_registry` | Decoded peer registry from Alfred |
| `/etc/mesh_ipv4_state` | Persistent IP chunk allocation |
| `/var/log/radio-setup.log` | radio-setup.sh output |
| `/boot/firmware/provision.log` | RPi provisioning log |
| `/var/log/mesh-provision.log` | Rock 3A provisioning log |

## Repo Structure

- `MANET/provisioning/` — Flashing scripts (`linux.sh`, `windows.ps1`) and first-boot templates
- `MANET/node_tools/` — Runtime scripts deployed to nodes (node-manager, elections, mesh tools)
- `MANET/binaries_arm64/` — Pre-compiled `alfred`, `batctl`, `wpa_supplicant_s1g` for ARM64
- `MANET/networkd-dispatcher/` — Carrier/routable event handlers for gateway management
- `MANET/install_packages/` — Tarball contents (kernel, firmware, systemd units)
- `MANET/systemd/` — Unit files for batman-enslave and its watchdog

## Default Credentials

| Account | Username | Password |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SSH / node user | `radio` | Set during provisioning |
| Armbian root (Rock 3A) | `root` | `1234` |

## SSH Notes

- User `radio` has sudo but requires password via `-S` flag in non-interactive sessions: `echo $PASSWORD | sudo -S <command>`
- `iw` and `batctl` are in `/usr/sbin/` which may not be in PATH; use full path or `export PATH=$PATH:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/sbin`

## Workflow Rules

- **Never commit on behalf of the user.** Make changes to files but leave `git add` and `git commit` to the user.
- **Always create a branch** before making code fixes. Use `git checkout -b <branch-name>` before editing files. Branch names should be descriptive (e.g. `fix/contiguous-wlan-link-naming`).
- **Keep live nodes and repo in sync.** When fixing something on a node via SSH, always make the corresponding fix in the repo on a branch in the same session. The repo is the source of truth — a live-only fix will be lost on the next provision or update.
